Executes a one-off C# script in the Unity Editor using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Scripting for immediate, quick Editor automation. Captures and returns logs generated during script execution. The C# scripting package must be available in Unity. The script must finish quickly and must not block the Unity main thread (see the `script` parameter for the exact no-wait/no-blocking constraints). Supports inline script code or loading from a file path. If the script requires the Unity/Tuanjie Editor to be in edit mode (i.e. it relies on AssetDatabase, scene editing, or any operation that is unsafe or unavailable while playing), you MUST first ensure the Editor is NOT in play mode using the unity_editor tool. Call unity_editor with action "get_state" to check whether the Editor is currently playing; if it is, call unity_editor with action "stop", which returns once play mode has actually exited, then re-run "get_state" to confirm it is no longer playing before executing this script. Only invoke "play" if you actually need play mode. If your script modifies an open scene (creating/destroying GameObjects, adding/removing components, or changing any property), you MUST mark the scene dirty and then save it at the end of the script, otherwise the changes are discarded on scene switch:var scene = UnityEditor.SceneManagement.EditorSceneManager.GetActiveScene();
UnityEditor.SceneManagement.EditorSceneManager.MarkSceneDirty(scene);
UnityEditor.SceneManagement.EditorSceneManager.SaveScene(scene);

